The correct system is not a knowledge graph of topics. It is an **inference-audit engine** organized around the pressure ladder.

Its atomic research object is:

[
\boxed{
\text{an attempted transition from one established claim to a stronger claim}
}
]

The system should make it impossible for an agent to silently move from:

[
\text{organisation}
]

to:

[
\text{phenomenal unity}
]

or from:

[
\text{reflexivity}
]

to:

[
\text{universal consciousness}
]

without explicitly storing the missing premise.

# FRONTIER PRESSURE GRAPH

## Formal Research Specification

---

# 1. Purpose

The Frontier Pressure Graph is a permanent, auditable system for advancing a philosophical enquiry through contested inferential transitions.

It preserves the established pressure sequence:

[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
P_01&=\text{Manifestation}\
P_02&=\text{Organisation}\
P_03&=\text{Perspective}\
P_04&=\text{Presence}\
P_05&=\text{Co-presence}\
P_06&=\text{Persistence}\
P_07&=\text{Ownership}\
P_08&=\text{Agency}\
P_09&=\text{Universalisation}\
P_{10}&=\text{Freedom}
\end{aligned}
}
]

The system does not assume that every pressure follows from the previous one.

Instead, every edge is treated as a disputed research problem:

[
E_n:
P_n
\stackrel{?}{\longrightarrow}
P_{n+1}.
]

The main task is to determine:

[
\boxed{
\text{What exact premise would make this transition valid?}
}
]

---

# 2. Governing principles

## 2.1 Preserve the pressure ladder

Agents may add:

* subpressures;
* branch pressures;
* backward dependencies;
* alternative paths;
* cross-pressure arguments.

They may not casually replace the ten-pressure ladder.

The ladder is the stable coordinate system of the enquiry.

---

## 2.2 Arguments, not theories, are the primary units

The system does not store:

> IIT says consciousness is integrated information.

It stores:

[
A_1,A_2,\ldots,A_n
\rightarrow
C.
]

Every argument must separate:

1. what the source explicitly claims;
2. what the reconstructed argument claims;
3. what the premises validly establish;
4. what remains unsupported.

---

## 2.3 Every stronger claim must expose its bridge

Suppose:

[
A\rightarrow B.
]

The system must store:

* (A): antecedent;
* (B): consequent;
* (H): hidden or bridging premise;
* (K): strongest countermodel.

The operative structure is:

[
A+H\rightarrow B.
]

If (H) is missing or unsupported, the edge remains open.

---

## 2.4 Countermodels have priority

A countermodel is not merely an objection.

It is a model satisfying the antecedent while denying the consequent:

[
K\models A
]

and:

[
K\not\models B.
]

If such a model is coherent, then:

[
A\not\Rightarrow B.
]

This is the system’s primary method for detecting invalid transitions.

---

## 2.5 Text, evidence and reconstruction must remain distinct

The graph must never merge:

* a Sanskrit passage;
* a translation;
* a scholar’s interpretation;
* an agent’s formal reconstruction;
* an empirical result;
* a metaphysical inference.

Each receives a separate object and provenance chain.

---

## 2.6 Checkpoints are compiled, not authored freely

A checkpoint is generated from the argument graph.

It may contain only:

* conclusions marked as surviving;
* explicit failure boundaries;
* unresolved bridge premises;
* live countermodels;
* newly generated frontier pressures.

A checkpoint cannot promote a claim merely because an agent wrote it persuasively.

---

# 3. Core graph

The graph contains seven main object classes:

[
\boxed{
\text{Pressure},
\text{Claim},
\text{Inference},
\text{Argument},
\text{Source},
\text{Evidence},
\text{ResearchBranch}.
}
]

Supporting objects include:

[
\text{Countermodel},
\text{Discriminator},
\text{Term},
\text{Passage},
\text{Checkpoint},
\text{AgentRun}.
]

