# 47. Final system theorem

The Frontier Pressure Graph is governed by:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{No conclusion advances merely because it is coherent,}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{textually prestigious, phenomenologically compelling}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\textbf{or scientifically fashionable.}
}
]

It advances only when:

[
\boxed{
\text{antecedent}
+
\text{explicit bridge}
+
\text{source support}
+
\text{countermodel survival}
\rightarrow
\text{consequent}.
}
]

Every failure produces useful information:

[
\boxed{
\text{failed inference}
\rightarrow
\text{missing premise}
\rightarrow
\text{new frontier}.
}
]

That is the engine that generated the checkpoints informally.

The formal system makes it permanent:

[
\boxed{
\text{pressure}
\rightarrow
\text{argument}
\rightarrow
\text{countermodel}
\rightarrow
\text{failure boundary}
\rightarrow
\text{discriminator}
\rightarrow
\text{new pressure}.
}
]

The graph does not attempt to store “the truth.”

It stores:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{exactly how far each argument currently reaches.}
}
]

The strongest implementation choice is to begin with YAML files in Git rather than a database. That makes every inference readable, diffable and version-controlled. PostgreSQL or a graph database should come only after the schemas survive several real research cycles.
